  TargetTrace: A Self-Learning Multi-Encoder Cross-Attention Framework for Simultaneous Drug Target Identification and Potency Prediction                                                                                                                                         
                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                  
  ---                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                             
  1. Introduction                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                 
                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                  
  Drug discovery is one of the most resource-intensive endeavours in modern science, with average development timelines exceeding a decade and costs approaching $2.6 billion per approved therapeutic [1]. A central challenge in early-stage discovery is identifying which     
  biological targets a candidate compound modulates, and at what potency — information that determines both therapeutic utility and liability before expensive wet-laboratory assays are undertaken. Computational methods for predicting drug-target interactions (DTI) therefore
   occupy a critical position in the discovery pipeline.                                                                                                                                                                                                                          
                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                  
  Classical quantitative structure-activity relationship (QSAR) approaches represent compounds as fixed-length descriptor vectors and learn target-specific regressors, but are inherently single-target and unable to generalise across the protein space [2]. The emergence of  
  deep learning enabled more expressive molecular representations, first through convolutional networks over fingerprint matrices [3], and subsequently through graph neural networks (GNNs) operating directly on the molecular graph [4,5]. These models substantially improved
  predictive performance for known targets, yet they continued to treat molecular structure in isolation from protein structure.                                                                                                                                                  
                                                            
  Parallel advances in protein representation learning — culminating in transformer-based protein language models pre-trained on hundreds of millions of sequences — have produced embeddings of remarkable quality that capture evolutionary, structural, and functional         
  information simultaneously [6,7]. Models such as ESM-2 [7] encode per-residue contextual representations that implicitly encode secondary and tertiary structural propensities, enabling downstream tasks including fold prediction and functional site annotation without
  requiring experimental structure determination.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                 
                                                            
  The integration of molecular and protein representations into a joint DTI model has been explored through several architectures. Concatenation-based fusion of pre-computed features forms the simplest baseline [8]; graph-based message passing over heterogeneous            
  molecule-protein graphs provides a more expressive alternative [9]; and cross-attention mechanisms drawn from natural language processing have recently been applied to learn residue-level interaction patterns between a ligand and its binding site [10,11]. However, most
  existing frameworks either discard per-residue protein information in favour of global pooled embeddings, use a single molecular representation, or lack mechanisms for online incorporation of newly measured data.                                                            
                                                            
  We present TargetTrace, a multi-encoder cross-attention architecture that simultaneously addresses target identification (multi-class classification across hundreds of protein classes) and potency prediction (pIC50 regression, differentiated per target) within a single   
  forward pass. TargetTrace fuses three orthogonal molecular representations — rule-based circular fingerprints, a transformer-derived contextual SMILES embedding, and a graph convolutional encoding of the atom-bond topology — with per-residue ESM-2 protein language model
  embeddings via a cross-attention module. The system is implemented as an interactive web application with a self-learning loop that continuously incorporates new ChEMBL data, re-trains from the latest checkpoint, and expands its output heads to accommodate newly          
  encountered targets without retraining from scratch. To our knowledge, TargetTrace is the first publicly interactive system to combine these four concurrent representation streams with a self-learning deployment interface.

  ---
  2. Methods

  2.1 Data Acquisition and Curation

  Bioactivity data were sourced from the ChEMBL database (release 33) [12] via the chembl_webresource_client Python API. Records were retrieved for 472 human single-protein targets spanning kinases, GPCRs, nuclear receptors, ion channels, proteases, and epigenetic enzymes. 
  For each target, bioactivity measurements of types IC₅₀, Kᵢ, K_d, and EC₅₀ were retained; only records with a standard unit of nanomolar (nM) and an equality relation ("=") were included to ensure comparability across assay formats.
                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                  
  Potency was expressed as the logarithmic pIC50 transform:                                                                                                                                                                                                                       
  
  $$\text{pIC}{50} = 9 - \log{10}([\text{activity}]_{\text{nM}})$$                                                                                                                                                                                                                
                                                            
  This transformation normalises the dynamic range, maps nanomolar activity (pIC₅₀ ≈ 9) through micromolar activity (pIC₅₀ ≈ 3), and renders potency directly interpretable relative to the 1 nM and 1 µM pharmacological thresholds. After deduplication by SMILES–target pair,  
  the final dataset comprised 848,315 records covering 472 protein targets and 222,371 unique canonical SMILES strings. Training data are stored in a local SQLite database (bioactivity.db) with provenance tracking and a trained flag to support incremental updates.
                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                  
  Protein amino acid sequences were retrieved via the UniProt REST API using the UniProt accession numbers associated with each ChEMBL target record.                                                                                                                             
  
  2.2 Molecular Representation                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                    
                                                            
  TargetTrace encodes each molecule using three independently trained branches whose outputs are subsequently fused.                                                                                                                                                              
                                                            
  Fingerprint branch. A 4,273-dimensional fixed-length descriptor vector is computed for each SMILES string using RDKit [13]. This vector concatenates: ECFP4 circular fingerprints (2,048 bits, Morgan radius 2) [14], capturing local atomic environments; ECFP6 fingerprints   
  (2,048 bits, Morgan radius 3), capturing longer-range topological context; MACCS structural keys (167 bits) [15], encoding presence or absence of expert-defined pharmacophoric fragments; and ten physicochemical descriptors (logP, topological polar surface area,
  nitrogen/oxygen count, molecular weight, hydrogen bond donors and acceptors, rotatable bonds, aromatic ring count, fraction sp³ carbon, and molar refractivity). Fingerprints are binary and require no model inference, making them both computationally cheap and             
  interpretable.                                            

  Contextual SMILES branch. ChemBERTa (seyonec/ChemBERTa-zinc-base-v1) [16], a RoBERTa-based transformer pre-trained on 500 million SMILES strings from the ZINC database, is used to produce a 768-dimensional [CLS] token embedding that captures long-range dependencies in the
   SMILES string. To reduce sensitivity to the arbitrary atom ordering imposed by SMILES notation, SMILES augmentation is applied at training time: for each canonical SMILES, up to N = 2 additional random SMILES representations of the same molecule are generated using
  RDKit's MolToSmiles(doRandom=True, canonical=False) [17]. All variants are pre-computed and cached; during training, one variant is sampled uniformly at random per epoch, forcing the model to learn chemistry invariant to canonical ordering rather than memorising token    
  positions.                                                

  Graph convolutional branch. Each molecule is converted to a fully connected padded graph with a maximum of 50 heavy atoms (accounting for 94% of the ChEMBL dataset). Atom features (41-dimensional per atom) encode atom type (12 element classes plus "other"), degree, formal
   charge, hybridisation state, aromaticity, ring membership, and total hydrogen count — each represented as a one-hot vector with an explicit "other" bucket. Bond weights are encoded in the adjacency matrix as 1.0 (single), 1.5 (aromatic), 2.0 (double), and 3.0 (triple).
  The adjacency matrix is symmetrically normalised as $\tilde{A} = D^{-1/2} A D^{-1/2}$ with self-loops (where $D$ is the degree matrix), following the formulation of Kipf and Welling [18].                                                                                     
                                                            
  A three-layer Graph Convolutional Network (GCN) processes the graph:

  $$H^{(l+1)} = \sigma!\left(\tilde{A} H^{(l)} W^{(l)}\right)$$                                                                                                                                                                                                                   
  
  with layer widths [41 → 128 → 128 → 256]. Batch normalisation and ReLU activations are applied after each layer except the last. A masked global pooling strategy concatenates the mean-pooled and max-pooled representations over real (non-padding) atoms, and a linear       
  projection maps the 512-dimensional concatenation back to 256 dimensions.
                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                  
  The three branches are concatenated (768-dimensional total) and passed through a two-layer fusion MLP:                                                                                                                                                                          
  
  $$\mathbf{m} = \text{MLP}\text{mol}([\mathbf{f}\text{fp};, \mathbf{f}\text{bert};, \mathbf{f}\text{gnn}]) \in \mathbb{R}^{256}$$                                                                                                                                                
                                                            
  2.3 Protein Representation                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                      
                                                            
  Protein sequences are encoded using ESM-2 (facebook/esm2_t12_35M_UR50D) [7], a 34 M-parameter transformer pre-trained on 250 million UniRef50 sequences via masked language modelling. ESM-2 produces a 480-dimensional contextual embedding for each residue position. Two     
  derived representations are used:
                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                  
  1. Global pooled embedding: mean-pooling across all residue positions yields a 480-dimensional vector summarising the overall sequence composition and structural propensity, analogous to a protein-level "fingerprint".                                                       
  2. Per-residue sequence: the first 128 residue embeddings (truncated from the maximum 512 cached) are retained as a (128 × 480)-dimensional matrix. These capture the spatially distributed information necessary for residue-level interaction modelling.
                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                  
  Both representations are pre-computed on first encounter and cached to disk as pickled arrays. Subsequent training and inference epochs incur no re-embedding cost; only novel sequences trigger on-the-fly ESM-2 inference. This design reduces the pre-computation bottleneck 
  to a one-time cost that amortises across all downstream training runs.                                                                                                                                                                                                          
                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                  
  2.4 Cross-Attention Mechanism                             

  The central architectural innovation of TargetTrace is the cross-attention module that conditions the molecular representation on the protein sequence context. Given the molecule query $\mathbf{q} = \mathbf{m}$ (256-dimensional, from Section 2.2), the per-residue protein 
  embeddings are first projected to a common dimension:
                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                  
  $$K = V = \text{Linear}\text{KV}(H\text{prot}) \in \mathbb{R}^{L \times 256}$$                                                                                                                                                                                                  
  
  where $H_\text{prot} \in \mathbb{R}^{L \times 480}$ is the residue embedding matrix and $L \leq 128$. Multi-head cross-attention with 8 heads is then applied:                                                                                                                  
                                                            
  $$\mathbf{a} = \text{MultiheadAttention}(\mathbf{q}, K, V, \text{mask}) \in \mathbb{R}^{256}$$                                                                                                                                                                                  
                                                            
  A key-padding mask excludes padded positions (residues beyond the actual sequence length) from the softmax normalisation, ensuring attention weights are computed only over real residues. The output is normalised via a residual LayerNorm [19]:                              
                                                            
  $$\mathbf{a} \leftarrow \text{LayerNorm}(\mathbf{a})$$                                                                                                                                                                                                                          
                                                            
  This mechanism allows the model to learn which protein residues are most relevant to a given molecule's interaction, effectively implementing a soft-attention over the binding environment.                                                                                    
                                                            
  2.5 Multi-Task Output Heads and Training Objective                                                                                                                                                                                                                              
                                                            
  The final representation is formed by concatenating the molecule representation $\mathbf{m}$, the global protein representation $\mathbf{p} = \text{MLP}\text{prot}(\mathbf{e}\text{pool})$, and the cross-attention output $\mathbf{a}$:                                       
                                                            
  $$\mathbf{z} = \text{MLP}_\text{fuse}([\mathbf{m};, \mathbf{p};, \mathbf{a}]) \in \mathbb{R}^{128}$$                                                                                                                                                                            
                                                            
  where the fusion MLP has architecture [768 → 512 → 256 → 128] with ReLU activations, batch normalisation, and dropout ($p = 0.2$). Two parallel output heads are applied to $\mathbf{z}$:                                                                                       
                                                            
  $$\hat{\mathbf{y}}\text{cls} = W\text{cls},\mathbf{z} \in \mathbb{R}^N$$                                                                                                                                                                                                        
  $$\hat{\mathbf{p}} = W_\text{pot},\mathbf{z} \in \mathbb{R}^N$$
                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                  
  where $N$ is the number of target classes. The target classification head produces logits for all $N$ proteins simultaneously; the potency head produces an independent pIC50 estimate for each protein class. During inference, the top-$k$ predicted targets are returned     
  alongside their associated pIC50 estimates, making predictions explicitly per-target rather than global.
                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                  
  The composite training loss is:                           

  $$\mathcal{L} = \mathcal{L}\text{CE}(\hat{\mathbf{y}}\text{cls},, y) + 0.3\cdot\mathcal{L}\text{Huber}(\hat{p}{y},, p)$$                                                                                                                                                        
  
  where $y$ is the true target class index, $p$ is the normalised pIC50 (z-scored using dataset mean and standard deviation), and $\hat{p}_y$ selects the potency prediction for the correct target. The Huber loss ($\delta = 1.0$) down-weights outliers relative to mean       
  squared error, improving robustness to noisy potency measurements. The 0.3 coefficient was set empirically to balance the scales of the two loss terms.
                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                  
  Class imbalance across the 472 targets (count range: tens to thousands of records per class) is addressed through two complementary mechanisms: (1) inverse-frequency class weights in the cross-entropy loss, clamped at 10× the median weight to prevent extreme up-weighting 
  of near-empty classes; and (2) a WeightedRandomSampler that oversamples rare targets during epoch construction, ensuring that each training batch has approximately uniform target representation in expectation.
                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                  
  2.6 Training Protocol                                     

  All models were optimised using AdamW [20] with learning rate $10^{-4}$ and weight decay $10^{-4}$. A cosine annealing schedule [21] decays the learning rate from $10^{-4}$ to $10^{-6}$ over the training run. Gradient norms are clipped to 1.0 to prevent instability in    
  early epochs. Batch sizes of 32 and 64 were used for training and validation respectively. An 80/20 stratified train-validation split was applied with a fixed random seed. pIC50 values were globally normalised (z-score) using the dataset-wide mean and standard deviation;
  these statistics are stored alongside the model checkpoint and applied consistently at inference time.                                                                                                                                                                          
                                                            
  When CUDA is available, Automatic Mixed Precision (AMP) training is enabled via PyTorch's GradScaler and autocast, providing approximately 2–3× throughput improvement over full float32 training on modern GPU hardware, with negligible effect on final accuracy.             
  
  2.7 Self-Learning and Incremental Updates                                                                                                                                                                                                                                       
                                                            
  TargetTrace is designed to improve continuously as new experimental data become available. New bioactivity records — whether imported via the ChEMBL API, uploaded as CSV files, or entered manually — are stored in the SQLite database with a trained=0 flag. Subsequent      
  training runs read all records and fine-tune the model from the most recent saved checkpoint rather than re-initialising weights.
                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                  
  When new target classes are encountered that were absent from prior training runs, the expand_targets method grows both output heads:                                                                                                                                           
  
  W_new = [W_old | W_rand]                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                        
                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                  
  The existing learned weights for known targets are preserved exactly; only the rows corresponding to new classes are randomly initialised. This allows TargetTrace to assimilate new biological targets incrementally without catastrophic forgetting of previously learned     
  associations.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                   
                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                  
  2.8 Memory-Efficient Dataset Construction                 

  A key engineering challenge at scale is dataset memory management. A naïve implementation storing full per-residue ESM-2 arrays (shape 512 × 480 × 4 bytes ≈ 1 MB) in every sample of an 848k-record dataset would require approximately 840 GB of RAM — exceeding all practical
   system limits. TargetTrace instead maintains separate shared dictionaries indexed by protein sequence hash and SMILES string respectively. Each unique protein contributes a single (128 × 480) float16 array (~120 KB) to the protein dictionary regardless of how many
  bioactivity records reference it; each unique SMILES contributes a single entry to the molecule dictionary. Per-sample records store only string keys (~200 bytes each). With 472 unique proteins and 222k unique SMILES, the peak dataset memory footprint is reduced to       
  approximately 7 GB. GCN arrays and BERT embeddings are stored as float16 and cast to float32 at mini-batch assembly time.

  2.9 Implementation

  TargetTrace is implemented in Python 3.11 using PyTorch 2.0+ for all neural network components. Molecular operations use RDKit 2023.3. The interactive interface is built with Streamlit 1.35+. ESM-2 and ChemBERTa are accessed via Hugging Face Transformers and are unloaded 
  from GPU memory immediately after pre-computation to free VRAM for model training. The complete system runs within a single process using background threading to decouple the training loop from the Streamlit WebSocket server, ensuring interface responsiveness during
  multi-hour training runs. All source code and trained model checkpoints are stored locally; no cloud inference is required at deployment time.                                                                                                                                  
                                                            
