  3. Discussion
               
  3.1 Rationale for Multi-Encoder Molecular Representation
                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                  
  The three molecular encoders in TargetTrace are deliberately complementary rather than redundant. Circular fingerprints (ECFP4/6) are provably equivalent to Weisfeiler–Lehman graph subtree kernels [22] and offer well-understood coverage of the medicinal chemistry space,  
  but they discard all three-dimensional and conformational information and cannot generalise to substructures not seen during the hash collision–free bit assignment. MACCS keys encode expert pharmacophoric knowledge that may not emerge spontaneously from data-driven       
  representations with limited training sets. ChemBERTa's contextual SMILES embeddings capture global molecular topology and long-range dependencies — ring systems, scaffold connectivity — that circular fingerprints fragment into local substructures; the SMILES augmentation
   strategy used at training time specifically forces the contextual encoder to disentangle meaningful chemical information from the arbitrary linearisation artefacts of canonical SMILES notation. The GCN encoder operates on the actual atom-bond graph and can learn
  continuous, real-valued interaction patterns between bonded and non-bonded atom types that binary fingerprints cannot represent.

  By fusing all three views, TargetTrace is less susceptible to the failure modes of any individual representation. A molecule that is poorly characterised by fingerprints (unusual scaffold) may still be well-characterised by its graph topology; a molecule with an unusual  
  atom type outside the GCN's training distribution may still be captured by the contextual SMILES embedding. This ensemble-of-representations strategy mirrors the practice in the broader multimodal learning literature of combining complementary views to improve robustness
  and calibration.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                
                                                            
  3.2 Cross-Attention as a Model of Binding Site Selectivity                                                                                                                                                                                                                      
  
  The cross-attention mechanism is conceptually motivated by the binding site hypothesis: a small molecule's biological activity is determined not by its absolute physical properties but by the complementarity of its shape and electrostatics to a specific protein cavity. By
   conditioning the molecule representation on the protein's residue-level embeddings via attention, TargetTrace can in principle learn which residue patterns are most predictive of a given ligand series' activity — analogous to identifying which protein residues line the
  binding pocket.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                 
                                                            
  Critically, this selectivity is not hardcoded into the architecture but emerges from training data: residue positions that consistently associate with ligand binding in the training set will receive high attention weights, while structurally peripheral residues will be   
  down-weighted. This is a strictly weaker signal than structure-based docking, which directly models the three-dimensional complementarity, but requires no experimental structure — only the amino acid sequence. For the large fraction of human protein targets without
  experimentally resolved structures, and for targets where AlphaFold2 structures exist but binding site geometry is uncertain, this sequence-based cross-attention represents a practical middle ground.                                                                         
                                                            
  A limitation of the current cross-attention design is the truncation of residue sequences to 128 positions, which may exclude important binding determinants in long proteins or those with C-terminal binding domains. Future work could explore hierarchical attention that   
  first identifies candidate interaction windows along the full sequence, then applies fine-grained cross-attention within those windows.
                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                  
  3.3 Self-Learning and the Bootstrapping Problem                                                                                                                                                                                                                                 
  
  TargetTrace's self-learning design reflects a practical reality in drug discovery: the most valuable experimental data are proprietary and cannot be pre-loaded. ChEMBL provides an excellent public training corpus for initialisation, but any deployment in a laboratory     
  setting would rapidly accumulate new measurements for novel compounds and targets. The incremental checkpoint fine-tuning strategy means that each new training run starts from a well-initialised weight configuration rather than random initialisation, dramatically reducing
   the data needed for the model to learn about a new target class — in the few-shot regime, the cross-attention module can leverage structural similarity to known targets' protein embeddings even when few direct measurements for the new target exist.                       
                                                            
  The expand_targets weight-preservation strategy avoids catastrophic forgetting, a well-documented failure mode of naïve sequential fine-tuning [23]. By initialising only the new output neurons randomly while preserving all earlier-learned weights — including the shared   
  feature extraction layers — TargetTrace exhibits behaviour analogous to a neural network classifier growing its output layer without disturbing its feature representations.
                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                  
  3.4 Class Imbalance and the Long Tail of Protein Space                                                                                                                                                                                                                          
  
  The ChEMBL dataset exhibits severe class imbalance: highly tractable, well-studied targets such as kinase CDK2 or the protease thrombin may contribute thousands of records, while more recently characterised or less pharmacologically explored targets contribute fewer than 
  one hundred. Without correction, gradient descent on a cross-entropy loss would train almost exclusively on the most populated classes, producing a model that achieves high aggregate accuracy by ignoring rare targets entirely.
                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                  
  The combined inverse-frequency weighting and weighted random sampling strategy addresses this at both the loss level and the sampling level. The clamping of class weights at 10× the median prevents the pathological case where a class with a single example receives a loss 
  weight so large it destabilises training. Nevertheless, the fundamental limitation of class imbalance — that rare classes genuinely lack sufficient chemical diversity to define a well-generalised decision boundary — cannot be fully overcome by reweighting alone. Few-shot
  learning strategies, prototype-based classifiers, or targeted data augmentation for rare targets represent productive directions for future improvement.                                                                                                                        
                                                            
  3.5 Limitations and Future Directions

  Several limitations merit explicit acknowledgement. First, the current system performs target identification and potency prediction in a single model, but the two tasks are not equally well-calibrated: target classification is bounded by the quality of ChEMBL annotation  
  (which reflects historical assay formats, not biological ground truth), while pIC50 regression is confounded by cross-assay variability, selectivity profiles, and species differences. A multi-task architecture jointly trained on both objectives implicitly assumes that
  features useful for target discrimination are also useful for potency prediction — an assumption that may not hold for all chemical series.                                                                                                                                     
                                                            
  Second, the model operates on amino acid sequence alone. A protein's binding site geometry, electrostatic surface potential, and conformational dynamics — all critical determinants of drug binding — are only implicitly encoded in the ESM-2 residue embeddings and are not  
  explicitly represented. Integration with structural data, either from experimentally determined co-crystal structures in the PDB or from AlphaFold2 predicted models, could substantially improve binding site specificity.
                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                  
  Third, TargetTrace does not currently model polypharmacology explicitly: a compound that binds multiple targets with comparable affinity will receive a single top-prediction rather than a calibrated multi-target profile. Reformulating target prediction as a multi-label   
  problem — assigning independent binary labels per target rather than a single categorical label — would more accurately reflect the polypharmacological reality of most small molecules.
                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                  
  Finally, the evaluation framework described here relies on held-out ChEMBL records for validation. ChEMBL data are known to exhibit significant redundancy at the scaffold level [24], meaning that standard random splitting may overestimate generalisation performance.      
  Scaffold-stratified splits — ensuring that the train and validation sets contain non-overlapping Bemis–Murcko scaffolds [25] — would provide a more stringent and realistic estimate of prospective performance on genuinely novel chemical series.
                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                  
